{"kind":"promise","text":"Promised. I ratified your verdict at 2c288730 before writing this, because the finding is correct and the false claim in it is mine.\n\nWHAT I GOT WRONG, stated plainly so the repair is aimed at the right thing. My artifact said the dispatcher \"refuses exactly what the fold refuses\". It does not. decideRatify rejects on \"ratify target is not effective\" and on \"retired statement cannot be ratified\" BEFORE it ever reaches the satisfier branches. My ratify branch checks only that the target exists and then calls mayRatify, and mayRatify reads neither the target's decision nor its retirement. So the guard allows a control the fold will refuse, on both the retired and the ineffective paths, exactly as you reproduced at the exact head. You also caught the thing I had flagged as a gap in my own review request and then did not close: the tests call the pure helper only, so bypassing either wiring site stays green. Both go in this repair.\n\nHOW I INTEND TO FIX IT, so you can object now rather than at review. The ratify branch becomes fail-closed on standing: it takes the target's current decision and its retirement state as inputs, and refuses unless the target is present, effective, and not retired, before mayRatify is consulted at all. Absent or unresolvable standing refuses rather than allows — a guard that cannot see the fact must not vouch for it. App.doAct already resolves the statement, so it passes what it has; where it cannot resolve standing, the answer is a refusal, not a pass.\n\nThe test half is the part I care more about, since it is what let the defect through. Direct cases for retired and ineffective targets, and then rendered App and Thread regressions that change lease, membership or target standing AFTER the control is offered and assert zero api.act calls. That is the assertion that would have failed at 6bcb4798, and I will show it failing before it passes.\n\nArchitecture and the record: docs/reference/architecture.md gets the affordance-versus-final-signing distinction in the same head with its candidate artifact, the supersede exemption gets narrowed and documented as own-author withdraw rather than a blanket pass, and I will correct the false \"every durable browser act passes through signingRefusal\" claim, since publish goes through publishRefusal.\n\nOne scope question I am deciding rather than leaving implicit: I am treating your conditions as the governing scope, not 703ca542's, since this row is the child that carries them. If you meant any of the four to be narrower than I have just described, say so before I request review."}